WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.659 --> 00:00:06.419
Speaker 1: The New York Times is a very interesting
report about more than 100 Republicans, including

00:00:06.419 --> 00:00:11.880
former Republican officials who are seriously
looking at abandoning the Republican Party

00:00:11.880 --> 00:00:13.889
and starting their own third party.

00:00:13.889 --> 00:00:17.970
There's a great article by Zachmanoglou published
late yesterday, I believe, in The New York

00:00:17.970 --> 00:00:22.761
Times, where he says more than 100 Republicans,
including some former elected officials, are

00:00:22.761 --> 00:00:28.070
preparing to release a letter this week threatening
to form a third party if the GOP doesn't make

00:00:28.070 --> 00:00:29.869
certain changes.

00:00:29.869 --> 00:00:34.370
The statement is going to take aim at former
President Trump's stranglehold on Republicans.

00:00:34.370 --> 00:00:42.130
The timing of this could not be more relevant,
given that with the ouster of Liz Cheney,

00:00:42.130 --> 00:00:46.809
Republican congresswoman from leadership,
Republicans in the House seem to be committing

00:00:46.809 --> 00:00:51.530
to a Trump in twenty twenty two platform.

00:00:51.530 --> 00:00:59.000
The preamble to this letter says, quote, When
in our Democratic Republic forces of conspiracy,

00:00:59.000 --> 00:01:05.320
division and despotism arise, it is the patriotic
duty of citizens to act collectively in defense

00:01:05.320 --> 00:01:07.430
of liberty and justice.

00:01:07.430 --> 00:01:13.640
And this is a first step, says Myles Taylor,
who is an organizer of the effort, if you

00:01:13.640 --> 00:01:14.990
recognize that name.

00:01:14.990 --> 00:01:20.190
That's because Taylor is the former Trump
era Department of Homeland Security official

00:01:20.190 --> 00:01:24.310
who anonymously wrote that book condemning
the Trump administration.

00:01:24.310 --> 00:01:28.760
He has since acknowledged that what was originally
written, anonymous anonymously was written

00:01:28.760 --> 00:01:30.240
by him.

00:01:30.240 --> 00:01:35.080
He goes on to say, quote, This is us saying
that a group of more than 100 prominent Republicans

00:01:35.080 --> 00:01:39.570
think that the situation has gotten so dire
with the Republican Party that it is now time

00:01:39.570 --> 00:01:43.300
to seriously consider whether an alternative
might be the only option now.

00:01:43.300 --> 00:01:46.520
We don't yet know the full scope of who else
is signing this letter.

00:01:46.520 --> 00:01:49.210
At some point we will when the letter is released.

00:01:49.210 --> 00:01:56.451
I am not optimistic that this group of hundred
Republicans can actually make a dent in the

00:01:56.451 --> 00:01:59.320
pro Trump Republican Party of right now.

00:01:59.320 --> 00:02:01.100
And here's what I mean by that.

00:02:01.100 --> 00:02:07.150
We've looked at polling that tells us the
voters right now are basically with Trump.

00:02:07.150 --> 00:02:13.120
70 percent of them still believe that Donald
Trump won something like two thirds said they

00:02:13.120 --> 00:02:18.560
would leave the Republican Party and follow
Trump if Trump were to start his own party.

00:02:18.560 --> 00:02:21.020
Now, I don't think two thirds really would.

00:02:21.020 --> 00:02:24.750
Two thirds are saying that they would, some
wouldn't, because they would realize actually,

00:02:24.750 --> 00:02:27.060
you know, maybe I don't want to follow Trump.

00:02:27.060 --> 00:02:29.280
Part of that two thirds wouldn't just because
of laziness.

00:02:29.280 --> 00:02:32.090
It actually takes some effort to leave the
Republican Party.

00:02:32.090 --> 00:02:35.550
Some would leave the Republican Party and
follow Trump, but would still mostly vote

00:02:35.550 --> 00:02:36.550
for Republicans.

00:02:36.550 --> 00:02:37.890
So, you know, we have to understand.

00:02:37.890 --> 00:02:43.080
That that two thirds is not if Trump leaves,
Republicans really lose two thirds of their

00:02:43.080 --> 00:02:44.080
votes.

00:02:44.080 --> 00:02:48.160
But although Trump seems to have meant millions
and tens of millions of Republicans, that

00:02:48.160 --> 00:02:50.190
would follow him out.

00:02:50.190 --> 00:02:58.420
It's not clear to me that if a hundred Republican
officials leave the party that that many voters

00:02:58.420 --> 00:03:04.770
follow them, I think that whatever happens
with the Republican Party in 2022 and 2024

00:03:04.770 --> 00:03:10.760
is going to happen from within the Republican
Party unless it's Trump who bails.

00:03:10.760 --> 00:03:11.790
Few disagree with me.

00:03:11.790 --> 00:03:13.100
I would love to hear from you.

00:03:13.100 --> 00:03:19.490
I just don't see any serious willingness from
Republican voters to say, particularly because

00:03:19.490 --> 00:03:21.640
they're mostly with Trump.

00:03:21.640 --> 00:03:25.610
If some officials leave, I will go with them.

00:03:25.610 --> 00:03:31.140
The counterpoint to this is that not that
many have to leave in order to potentially

00:03:31.140 --> 00:03:34.520
completely sabotage the chances of Republicans
in 2020.

00:03:34.520 --> 00:03:40.600
To many of these 20, 22 races are going to
be very tight, particularly in the Senate,

00:03:40.600 --> 00:03:42.630
but in the House as well.

00:03:42.630 --> 00:03:48.790
And even if it's true that 70, 75, 80 percent
of Republicans would not even consider leaving

00:03:48.790 --> 00:03:54.880
just because Miles Taylor is starting some
third party, and I think that that's the case.

00:03:54.880 --> 00:04:01.260
If only a few of them do leave and they are
disproportionately voting Republicans because

00:04:01.260 --> 00:04:05.880
remember, particularly in midterms, sometimes
half or more of the country doesn't even vote.

00:04:05.880 --> 00:04:12.140
It doesn't take that much to potentially completely
upend the chances of Republicans at taking

00:04:12.140 --> 00:04:15.150
back the House or the Senate in twenty twenty
two.

00:04:15.150 --> 00:04:17.489
So we have to sort of hold both things.

00:04:17.489 --> 00:04:22.060
On the one hand, it is very clear that it's
Trump who holds the cards here.

00:04:22.060 --> 00:04:24.120
Trump is still fundraising.

00:04:24.120 --> 00:04:29.550
Trump is recreating his communications with
the party, although to a very limited degree

00:04:29.550 --> 00:04:30.550
of success.

00:04:30.550 --> 00:04:35.750
Those blog posts he's making are only getting
a couple hundred thousand views total.

00:04:35.750 --> 00:04:40.780
And it is still a Republican Party in the
House of Representatives in the Senate that

00:04:40.780 --> 00:04:44.300
privately many of them despise privately.

00:04:44.300 --> 00:04:47.960
Maybe many of them despise Trump or don't
like the direction he's taking the party or

00:04:47.960 --> 00:04:49.040
whatever.

00:04:49.040 --> 00:04:54.800
But they clearly have calculated if and until
it becomes clear that the voters are no longer

00:04:54.800 --> 00:04:55.800
with Trump.

00:04:55.800 --> 00:05:02.410
I need to stay with Trump publicly for self-preservation
to avoid getting the the Trump dagger of saying

00:05:02.410 --> 00:05:04.400
that person needs to be voted out or whatever.

00:05:04.400 --> 00:05:07.770
The case may be so good for these Republican
officials.

00:05:07.770 --> 00:05:10.140
I don't think this is going to have much of
an impact whatsoever.

00:05:10.140 --> 00:05:11.720
I'd love to hear from you about it.

